  "title": "Form Elements in JSX",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Course: Build your First Web App with React"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Form Elements in JSX"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Form elements work the same in JSX as in HTML, with a few small differences:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "1. 'class' becomes 'className'"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You already know this one:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "<input className=\"title-input\" />"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "2. Self-closing tags need a slash"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In HTML, you can write <input> or <input />. In JSX, you must close it like this: <input />."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "3. camelCase for attributes"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Some HTML attributes combine two words without camelCasing (eg: tabindex, maxlength). In JSX, they become camelCase:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "tabindex → tabIndex",
        "maxlength → maxLength",
        "readonly → readOnly"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "For example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "<input type=\"text\" maxLength={100} />"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "4. 'for' becomes 'htmlFor'"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If you use <label>, the for attribute becomes htmlFor:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "react",
      "content": "<label htmlFor=\"title\">Title</label>\n\n<input id=\"title\" />"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Why? Because for is a reserved word in JavaScript (used in for loops)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
